No matter which stage of the game we're talking about, there's almost
certainly *some* space to explore between "warning doesn't distinguish
between threats as divergent as a lizard and mind flayer" and "warning
is essentially a fully-functioning replacement for ESP." :)

That said, if the purpose is to make warning weaker, especially as the
game goes on, then the change serves that purpose, and I suppose
that's one legitimate avenue to making the game more challenging. I'm
skeptical that this won't have a similar effect on early game
characters, though. Obviously there it wouldn't be lizards and mind
flayers, but it might be be rothes and soldier ants.

But who knows? A wider scale might be easier and more like vanilla
warning, but if you were to actually implement a sufficient corner
case list, that could make warning *significantly* more useful in the
early game. Distinguish between a goblin with a wand of death and a
fox, could make the difference between a breathless but interesting
start and... starting over.

